# git — worktrees (multiple checkouts of one repo)

0.1.0 Use when you need to have two branches checked out simultaneously — review a PR without losing in-progress work, run parallel tests across branches, sub-agent isolation. `git worktree` instead of clone.

- Switch to a PR to review without losing in-progress work
on a different branch (faster than stash + checkout + work + checkout-back)
- Run a build/test on branch A while you keep editing branch B
- Spawn a sub-agent on its own branch without contaminating
your main checkout (the Workbooks runtime uses this pattern natively — see `SubAgent.maybe_create_worktree`)

NOT for: just switching branches (use `git switch`); needing totally independent copies (use `git clone`); shared collaboration across users (use remotes).

app :git__worktrees_multiple_checkouts_of_one_repo do
  title       "git — worktrees (multiple checkouts of one repo)"
  description "0.1.0 Use when you need to have two branches checked out simultaneously — review a PR without losing in-progress work, run parallel tests across branches, sub-agent isolation."
end

## The model

`git worktree` makes a NEW working directory tied to the SAME `.git/` storage. You get N filesystem dirs, each on its own branch, sharing one object database. Switching between them is just `cd`.

Disk cost: only the working files duplicate; the objects (which dominate repo size) stay shared.

## Workflow

### Create a worktree

### 1. verify the branch doesn't already have a worktree
```bash
  git worktree list
  # if the branch you want appears, just cd into its path
```

### 2a. worktree at a new path on an existing branch
```bash
  git worktree add ../my-pr-review pr-branch-123
  cd ../my-pr-review
```

### 2b. worktree + create a new branch in one step
```bash
  git worktree add -b feature/x ../wt-feature-x
  cd ../wt-feature-x
```

### List

### see all live worktrees
```bash
  git worktree list
  # /Users/.../my-repo               abc1234 [main]
  # /Users/.../my-pr-review          def5678 [pr-branch-123]
```

### Remove

### clean up a worktree you're done with
```bash
  git worktree remove ../wt-feature-x
  # refuses if the worktree has uncommitted changes; pass --force to override
```

### prune stale worktree entries (after manual rm -rf)
```bash
  git worktree prune
  # walks .git/worktrees/ and removes orphans
```

## Sub-agent worktree pattern (Workbooks-specific)

The runtime's SubAgent supervisor creates one worktree per child agent at `<repo>/.workbooks/worktrees/<child-id>` on branch `oql/<child-id>`. Each child runs isolated; parent `merge_back/3` consolidates results.

### see what sub-agent worktrees exist
```bash
  ls -la .workbooks/worktrees/
  git worktree list | grep workbooks
```

## Common pitfalls

1. *Submodules + worktrees interact weirdly.* Submodules need
     to be re-init'd in each worktree. =git submodule update --init --recursive= after `git worktree add` if your repo has submodules. See [[submodule-flows]].

2. *Hooks live in `.git/hooks`, shared across worktrees.* Your
     pre-commit hook fires for ALL worktrees. If a hook needs per-worktree config, gate it on `$(git worktree list | head -1)` or move hook logic to a script the hook delegates to.

3. *Detached HEAD worktrees from `git worktree add HEAD ...`.*
     Without a branch arg, you get a detached HEAD in the new worktree. Either pass a branch (`-b new-name`) or check out one after creation.

4. *Force-removing a worktree with uncommitted work loses it.*
     `git worktree remove --force` doesn't stash. If you need to keep the work, commit / stash first.

5. *The main worktree (where `.git/` lives) can't be removed.*
     It's the parent of all sub-worktrees. Delete the whole repo to be rid of it.

## Verification checklist

- [ ] `git worktree list` shows the new worktree
- [ ] Each worktree on its intended branch
- [ ] No orphan dirs at the worktree path post-remove
- [ ] If sub-agent pattern: child branch starts from parent's HEAD

